# Enrich

A raw lead arrives as little more than a name. Before [[Score]] can rank it the
lead has to be **filled in** — its `domain`, `revenue`, and `employees` pulled
from our customer-record system. That outward reach to the CRM is exactly the
kind of thing the browser is not allowed to do on its own, which makes :enrich
the right place to teach **grants**.

## Placement: where this runs

This unit is **server** code. It runs as native Elixir on the BEAM in the nexus —
not as a wasm island in the browser. That matters for two reasons. First, the CRM
URL and its key are secrets; only the trusted server tier may hold them. Second,
reaching out over the network is a privileged act, and the server tier is where
the [[Dock]] can broker that privilege. A client unit could never call
`api.crm.com` directly — it would have no way to be granted the network.

## Grants are imports

The most important line in this file is the grant. In the work model a unit's
**dependencies and grants ARE its imports** — the capabilities the Dock must
fulfill before the unit may run. We never write WIT; we declare what we need in
Elixir and the contract falls out.

Here `grant net: "api.crm.com"` means: *this unit imports one capability — the
ability to make network calls, and only to that one host.* It is a capability
boundary, not a convenience. If :enrich tried to reach `evil.example` the Dock
would refuse the call, because that host was never granted. Grants flow **by
explicit grant, never by inheritance** — a unit that calls :enrich does not
inherit its net access.

server :enrich, grant: [net: "api.crm.com"] do
  @moduledoc """
  Fill a raw %Lead{} from the CRM: domain, revenue, employees, then mark it
  :enriched. The grant above is the only capability this unit imports — one
  network host, brokered through the Dock.
  """

  @crm_base "https://api.crm.com/v1"

  @doc """
  enrich(%Lead{}) -> %Lead{}

  Reaches out through the Dock to look the lead up by name, merges the CRM
  fields onto the struct, and advances its status to :enriched. On a lookup
  failure the lead is returned unchanged so the [[Pipeline]] can keep moving
  and a later pass can retry it.
  """
  def enrich(%Lead{name: name} = lead) do
    case lookup(name) do
      {:ok, record} ->
        %Lead{
          lead
          | domain: record.domain,
            revenue: record.revenue,
            employees: record.employees,
            status: :enriched
        }

      {:error, reason} ->
        # The CRM had nothing (or was unreachable). Leave the lead as-is;
        # status stays :new so a retry can pick it up next run.
        Logger.warning("enrich miss for #{name}: #{inspect(reason)}")
        lead
    end
  end

  # The actual reach-out. Dock.fetch is the brokered network call: the membrane
  # checks our net grant against this host before the request leaves the nexus.
  # The result is a tagged variant, {:ok, record} | {:error, reason}, which is
  # this unit's error type — no exceptions cross the boundary.
  defp lookup(name) do
    url = @crm_base <> "/accounts?name=" <> URI.encode(name)

    case Dock.fetch(url, headers: [{"accept", "application/json"}]) do
      {:ok, %{status: 200, body: body}} ->
        {:ok, parse(body)}

      {:ok, %{status: 404}} ->
        {:error, :not_found}

      {:ok, %{status: code}} ->
        {:error, {:http, code}}

      {:error, reason} ->
        {:error, {:net, reason}}
    end
  end

  # The CRM speaks JSON over the wire — that's a genuine network payload at a
  # boundary, the one place JSON is allowed. We decode it straight into a small
  # record (a struct, structure not DSL) and never let the raw map leak inward.
  defp parse(body) do
    fields = Jason.decode!(body)

    %{
      domain: Map.get(fields, "domain", ""),
      revenue: Map.get(fields, "annual_revenue", 0),
      employees: Map.get(fields, "headcount", 0)
    }
  end
end

## The result type, made explicit

Notice that `lookup/1` never raises. It returns a **tagged tuple variant** —
`{:ok, record} | {:error, reason}` — and `enrich/1` pattern-matches on it. This
is the work way to model fallibility: the structure of the value carries success
or failure, and the inferred type of `lookup/1` is precisely that union. The
`reason` is itself a small tagged value (`:not_found`, `{:http, code}`,
`{:net, reason}`) so a caller can tell *why* enrichment missed without parsing a
string.

## What comes next

A freshly enriched lead — `status: :enriched` — is exactly what [[Score]] expects
as input. The [[Pipeline]] wires the two together: it runs :enrich across the
seeded leads, then hands the filled structs to :score. Because :enrich is pure
apart from its one brokered call, the pipeline can run it in parallel across the
batch and the weave gate can audit that single `net` grant in isolation.

For the canonical inputs, `%Lead{name: "Globex", domain: "globex.io",
revenue: 12_000, employees: 8, status: :enriched}` is what a successful pass over
a raw Globex lead produces — the CRM filled the blanks and the status moved one
step along `@statuses`.
